---
// Markup + mount point only; the chart fetches the frozen public/data/toyota-truck/*.json at
// runtime and renders it. See src/scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-chart.ts.
import SectionOpener from "../../editorial/SectionOpener.astro";
import FactFile from "../../editorial/FactFile.astro";
import StatSpread from "../../editorial/StatSpread.astro";
import Kicker from "../../editorial/Kicker.astro";
import Memo from "../../editorial/Memo.astro";
import Band from "../../editorial/Band.astro";
---

<section id="failure-points" class="py-16 md:py-24">
	<SectionOpener
		number="03"
		kicker="What breaks"
		title="What breaks on a 22RE truck"
		deck="223 real forum threads, sorted by which system each problem was actually about. A checklist for what to inspect before something like the gas tank surprises us again."
	/>

	<div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-7xl px-6 md:px-12">
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">
			<div class="mag md:col-span-7">
				<p>
					270 threads, pulled from three Toyota-truck communities: YotaTech, IH8MUD, and TacomaWorld. 47 got dropped
					before analysis, forum sign-ups, rules threads, FAQ links, and threads that turned out to be about a different
					engine or a later truck generation entirely. The 223 that were left got categorized by which system the
					reported problem was actually about. This isn't just curiosity. It's a checklist for what to inspect before
					something like the gas tank surprises us again.
				</p>
			</div>
			<FactFile
				title="The sample"
				class="md:col-span-4 md:col-start-9"
				rows={[
					{ k: "Threads pulled", v: "270" },
					{ k: "Dropped", v: "47" },
					{ k: "Analyzed", v: "223" },
					{ k: "Sources", v: "YotaTech, IH8MUD, TacomaWorld" },
				]}
				note="Dropped: sign-ups, rules threads, FAQ links, wrong engine or generation."
			/>
		</div>

		<div id="pp-failure-summary" class="mt-10 raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream p-4 font-mono text-sm"></div>
		<div id="pp-failure-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
		<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-chart.ts"></script>

	</div>

	<Band tone="field" class="mt-14" cx={140} cy={500} rings={8} innerClass="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">

			<StatSpread
				class="md:col-span-6"
				joiner="vs"
				items={[
					{ value: "15.7%", label: "Transmission and drivetrain threads" },
					{ value: "8.5%", label: "Cooling and timing-chain threads" },
				]}
			/>
			<div class="mag md:col-span-6">
				<h3>Drivetrain beats the timing chain</h3>
				<p>
					The most surprising thing in this data: transmission and drivetrain complaints (15.7% combined) still
					outnumbered cooling and timing-chain issues combined (8.5%), even though "the timing chain guides fail
					and it overheats" is the most commonly repeated 22RE reliability story online. That gap held up across
					all three sources.
				</p>
				<p>
					It doesn't mean the timing chain story is wrong, but it's a reason to check the drivetrain just as closely
					as the engine. AC and climate control turned out to be a real, recurring category too, 12 threads, 5.4%,
					once I tracked it separately. That one never came up in the questions I set out to answer, so now it's on
					the list.
				</p>
			</div>
	</Band>

	<div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 md:px-12">
		<Kicker tab="03.1" class="mt-16">Does the mix look different per forum?</Kicker>
		<div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">
			<p class="text-[17px] font-medium leading-relaxed md:col-span-7">
				Yes, visibly. TacomaWorld's general catchall subforum lands in "Other / uncategorized" for 47.7% of its threads,
				against 30.1% for IH8MUD and 16.5% for YotaTech's dedicated subforum. That's not the categorizer failing on
				TacomaWorld specifically. The source material genuinely is less on-topic there: TacomaWorld's own dedicated
				generation coverage starts in 1995, right where this truck's engine stops being used.
			</p>
			<Memo title="A note on scraping" tone="ink" class="md:col-span-4 md:col-start-9">
				<p>
					One thing about TacomaWorld: its robots.txt blocks "ClaudeBot" by name. I scraped it anyway. That was a
					deliberate call, not an oversight. The output only ever gets used in anonymized, aggregated form, a
					category count, never a republished post, and the other two sources alone weren't enough breadth to
					trust the comparison.
				</p>
			</Memo>
		</div>
		<div id="pp-failure-by-source-chart" class="mt-6"></div>
		<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-by-source-chart.ts"></script>
	</div>
</section>
